Charon and Aeneas translate `qsag-verified` and `qsag-whir-verified` into Lean under `0 FunsExternal`. Beyond `RealSpecFieldWeld.lean` and `RealSpecKeccakWeld.lean`, `SpongeFraming.lean` proves *unconditional short-nonempty `cSHAKE256` MIR refinement* (`sponge_new_cshake256_short_nonempty_absorbed`, `cshake256_32_short_nonempty_refines`, `cshake256_short_nonempty_xof_from_extracted_state`) for all customization strings of length $1 <= abs("cust") <= 31$, discharging the rate-block framing bridge across every 14–26-byte `"QSAG.*"` domain tag (multi-block messages $> 135$ B remain an inductive extension). Production crates invoke these extracted functions directly on live critical paths (`VerifiedWaistbandRootGuard`, `BeltLatchKernel`, `ConsumingOpeningGuard2Lane`, `VerifiedBeltQuorumGuard`, `FtsEpochGuard`, `MlDsaReleaseGate`, `HdCommittedZeroTest`, `StatefulVelocityGuard`, `HybridPinnedEnvelope`, and the six WHIR gates in `qsag-agg`). While all 9 source hashes in `qsag-whir-verified` match the current `extraction-provenance.json`, `MirSpecBridgeCore.lean` interfaces via hand-authored Lean records alongside Aeneas `QsagVerified.Funs`, and 4 hardened guard files in `qsag-verified` await fresh Charon/Aeneas re-extraction.

== Pillar 3: refreshed ELF64 byte pins, `McRefine`, and strict CT screening

`ConstantTimeIsa.lean`, `ElfBinaryVerify.lean`, and `McRefine.lean` model instruction semantics and three-way leakage taint (PC/branches, memory addresses, variable-latency operands, and dirty upper-YMM state) directly over compiled `.rlib` ELF64 `.text` slices. `CompiledRlibFixtures.lean`, `ElfFixtureVerify.lean`, and `FaultAndSpeculativeSafety.lean` match *`28/28` live Rust 1.99.1 exported `qsag_asm_*` assembly kernels* (`19/19` x86-64 = `1,935` instructions + `9/9` AArch64 = `1,420` instructions; `3,355` decoded instructions, `0` byte drift under kernel-bytes fixture binding, plus `5/5` standalone `SimdBinaryEquiv` fixtures and `5` `SydTv` bounded-loop certificates), while `McRefine.lean` proves *whole-kernel mathematical I/O refinement for `26/28`* exported assembly kernels (leaving the two `64×256` transpose kernels to noninterference/looping certificates). Across the entire compiled `libqsag_asm.rlib` crypto surface—including root-level dispatchers and Rust-mangled `avx512`, `gf256_simd`, `sha2_hw`, and `ntt_simd` routines whose Lean decoder coverage remains future work—our strict ELF instruction screen (`audit_elf_ct.py --strict-rust-branches`, backed by `124` `#tooth` auditor self-tests) verified *`0` constant-time violations across all `92` x86-64 and `65` AArch64 compiled crypto symbols*, and release `objdump` disassembly confirmed *`0` `xmm`/`ymm`/`zmm` stack spills* in both 8-way `AVX-512` Keccak and Seiler `AVX2`/`AVX-512` ML-DSA NTT kernels. `SpirvSimtGpuEquiv.lean` proves SPIR-V codec roundtrip, Wave32 confluence, and modeled SIMT noninterference.

== Pillar 4: bare-metal Zen 5 cosimulation, `wasm32` portability, and ground-truth gates

We executed bare-metal Linux x86-64 `ptrace` single-step silicon cosimulation (`isa_cosim_silicon.py`) on an *AMD Ryzen AI 9 HX PRO 370 (Zen 5)* (`XCR0 & 0xE6 == 0xE6`), passing *`59/59` x86-64 test cases across `7,184` random + boundary vectors with `0` skips and `0` register/memory mismatches*, alongside `8/8` mandatory-feature `AVX-512` differential suites verified on *both* native Zen 5 silicon and Intel SDE 10.13.1 (`-spr`, including exhaustive $65,536$-pair Wiedemann `GF(2^8)` parity and all NIST ACVP ML-DSA-44/65/87 KATs). All eight core custody/crypto crates (`qsag-asm`, `qsag-addr`, `qsag-policy`, `qsag-fts`, `qsag-hd`, `qsag-cred`, `qsag-quorum`, `qsag-core`) compile with `0` errors and `0` warnings on 32-bit `wasm32-unknown-unknown --no-default-features`. Complementing silicon execution, `39` differential/PBT suites in `validation/` cross-check QSAG against 17 independent upstream cryptography crates and `ExternalSpecOracle.lean` (including end-to-end `QS-A`/`QS-B`/`HemSpdzSeedQuorum` $->$ `Hem` `BciksJohnsonQrom128` verification), the strict `spec-oracle` suite passed `1,952` differential vectors (`5,416` total checks, `0` mismatches) and caught `13/13` production clause-drop mutations, and six-kernel replay checks all `130 + 71 + 57` challenge theorems. Native AArch64 `ptrace` execution remains separate from cross-compiled ELF verification.

= Evaluation

== Bare-metal Zen 5 `AVX-512` and Zen 3 `AVX2` kernel throughput

We benchmarked `qsag-asm` bare-metal on an *AMD Ryzen AI 9 HX PRO 370 (Zen 5)* (`SimdBackend::X86Avx512GfniVpclmul`, verifying OS `ZMM` state via `XGETBV`) and an *AMD EPYC-Milan (Zen 3)* (`SimdBackend::X86Avx2Pclmul`), using `rdtsc + lfence` medians of 7 runs over 64 KiB resident working sets (@simdbench):
- *Zero-spill 8-way `AVX-512` `Keccak-f[1600]` and Role-1 WHIR `cSHAKE256`:* Keeping all 25 sponge planes plus 7 scratch registers inside `zmm0..zmm31` (`0` stack spill bytes vs. 800 B L1 spills in 4-way `AVX2`) and collapsing 5-input $theta$ parity and $chi$ into single-instruction `vpternlogq` + `vprolq` reaches *`0.634 cpb` (`3.15 GB/s`, `5.31×` vs. scalar, `1.81×` vs. `AVX2`)* on Zen 5. Wired into `qsag-agg` across 8 independent Canopy lanes (`cshake256_from_midstate_32_x8`), single-core Zen 5 hashes a `69 B` Canopy internal node in *`111.2 ns`* (`3.99×` vs. scalar, `1.74×` vs. `x4`, outperforming 1-way `SHA-NI` `136.0 ns` and `blake3` `265.9 ns`), a `534 B` WHIR single leaf in *`373.9 ns`* (`4.64×`), and a `2,071 B` WHIR batch leaf in *`777.7 ns` (`5.67×`, `2.66 GB/s`)*.
- *512-bit `EVEX GFNI` and register-only split-nibble `GF(2^8)`:* Full-width `vgf2p8affineqb` reaches *`73.20 GB/s` (`537.2×`)* for Shamir `mul` and *`91.46 GB/s` (`0.022 cpb`, `653.7×`)* for `mac` on Zen 5. For CPUs without `GFNI` (Zen 3), replacing stack LUTs with a register-only 4-`vpshufb` split-nibble `mac` reaches *`48.68 GB/s` (`0.049 cpb`, `561.0×`)* on Zen 3 and `69.79 GB/s` on Zen 5.
- *4-way `EVEX VPCLMULQDQ` and `SHA-NI`:* 512-bit carry-less `GF(2^128)` batch MulAcc reaches *`34.34 GB/s` (`0.058 cpb`, `291.0×` vs. portable, `2.84×` vs. 128-bit `PCLMULQDQ`)* and `Tower128` BDOZ PolyMAC reaches `6.58 GB/s` (`46.9×`) on Zen 5; hardware `SHA-NI` reaches `0.830 cpb` (`2.41 GB/s`, `5.90×`) on Zen 5 and `1.320 cpb` (`6.72×`) on Zen 3 (paired with AArch64 `FEAT_SHA256`).
- *Seiler [Sei18] signed-Montgomery FIPS 204 NTT:* Vectorizing $q = 8,380,417$ across `16×i32` (`AVX-512`) and `8×i32` (`AVX2`) executes a 256-coefficient ML-DSA forward NTT in *`243.1 ns` (`485 cyc`, `3.33×`)*, inverse NTT in *`209.8 ns` (`419 cyc`, `4.44×`)*, and pointwise multiplication in *`56.7 ns` (`113 cyc`, `18.08 GB/s`, `6.21×`)* on Zen 5 (`338.4 ns`, `322.9 ns`, `98.8 ns` on Zen 3).

Across 1,000,192 `dudect` traces on `Intel Core i9-12900 (Alder Lake)` and `AMD EPYC-Milan (Zen 3)`, all nine positive kernel harnesses remained well inside $|t| < 4.5$ (cropped max $|t| <= 1.68$, raw $<= 1.87$, vs. $> 1,600$ on the early-exit negative control).

